  <h1>The Diagnostic Window</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> The diagnostic window is the period after infection when a given marker is detectable — antigen early, antibody later, and PCR earliest.</p>
  <p>The period after infection during which a given test can detect it — and why choosing the wrong marker misses cases.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Marker</th><th>Detectable from</th><th>Indicates</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Antigen</th><td>Early (acute phase)</td><td>Active pathogen present</td></tr>
        <tr><th>IgM antibody</th><td>~days after onset</td><td>Recent infection</td></tr>
        <tr><th>IgG antibody</th><td>~weeks after onset</td><td>Past infection / immunity</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Nucleic acid (PCR)</th><td>Very early</td><td>Active infection (gold standard)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Practical rule</h2>
  <p>
    Test too early and the antibody may not have appeared; test too late and the antigen may be gone.
    Combined antigen+antibody devices widen the window. See
    <a href="/comparisons/dengue-ns1-vs-igg-igm/">Dengue NS1 vs IgM/IgG</a> for a worked example.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the window period?</strong> The time after infection before a given marker becomes detectable — antigen early, antibody later, PCR earliest.</p>
  </div>

